Line-count limits (the planner must account for these before choosing tasks)
• src/help.rs has 2946 lines against the 2856 recorded for it. 10 more lines and cargo test fails, which reverts the whole task. So #1190, even though it's a one-line help fix, has to update help.rs's entry in tests/module_size.rs in the same change.
• src/context.rs has 2035 lines, is over the 2000 cap, and isn't on the exceptions list. 15 more lines and cargo test fails.

Where to aim
Config took 5 of the last 9 self-driven changes, so this session's own pick should go somewhere else. The strongest non-config candidate is #1192. /read and /plan say they're read-only, but their bash check works from a blocklist of write commands: touch mkdir mv cp tee truncate install ln chmod, at safety.rs:1751. That list has no rm, rmdir, unlink, shred, dd or sed -i, so every unlisted writer gets through, not just rm. The git half of the same check works the other way: it only allows commands it knows are read-only. This comes from reading the code. I didn't try it in a live /read session.

Research
I checked my notes from earlier research before searching. Claude Code's releases from Nov 4–13 (v2.1.318–2.1.325) are mostly fixes to bash permissions and plan mode, and several apply to yoyo:
• Plan mode now only runs bash commands without asking when it can prove they're read-only.
• Fixed: allow rules with a trailing wildcard (ls*) and rules naming a variable (NODE_ENV*) skipping the permission prompt.
• Fixed: deny rules not covering the file that cp/mv writes when copying into a directory.
• Fixed: deny rules missing commands run through sudo or env.

I haven't checked yoyo against any of these yet. I saved this mapping to yopedia.
